
  [1mC. Hybrid — event log + per-user read state[0m
  The log from B, plus a small [38;5;153mnotification_state[39m table holding a per-user read cursor and a set of explicitly dismissed
  event IDs.
  - Wins: B's write model and re-derivable history, with A's per-item dismiss.
  - Costs: two concepts instead of one, and the dismissed-set needs pruning eventually.

  I considered and am [1mdiscarding a client-only version[0m (everything in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no server): it can't satisfy "team"
  — two people would never see each other's changes.

  [1mTransport is a separate, smaller axis.[0m Polling ([38;5;153mGET /api/notifications?since=[39m, every ~15s) versus server-sent events.
  I'd start with polling and leave SSE as a drop-in swap later: for a bell badge, 15-second latency is imperceptible,
  and polling costs roughly zero complexity while SSE brings connection lifecycle, reconnect, and proxy buffering with
  it. The API shape is identical either way, so this is genuinely deferrable.

  [1mMy recommendation: C, the hybrid.[0m B is the architecture I'd want, and its one real weakness is exactly the thing your
  users will touch most — clicking an item in a bell dropdown and expecting it to stop nagging them. The read-state
  table is maybe thirty lines and buys that back without giving up re-derivable history. If you'd rather cut scope, B
  alone with mark-all-read is a defensible v1 and C is a clean addition later; A is the one I'd steer away from, because
  we've [3malready[0m committed to changing the subscription rule (mute/watch in pass two), which is precisely the change A
  makes expensive.

  Two things I need from you before I write the design up:
